  "title": "Detect signals executed inside shell expansions",
  "file": "src/harness/signals.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An agent runs a kill command hidden inside a double-quoted command substitution, such as echo \"$(kill -TERM 123)\", or inside a heredoc fed to cat. The shell still runs the kill, but no warning reaches the event log, the feed or the report.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun -e 'import { signalCommand } from \"./src/harness/signals.ts\"; for (const c of [\"echo \\\"$(kill -TERM 123)\\\"\", \"cat <<EOF\\n$(kill 123)\\nEOF\", \"echo $(kill 123)\"]) console.log(JSON.stringify(c), signalCommand(c));'",
    "output": "\"echo \\\"$(kill -TERM 123)\\\"\" false\n\"cat <<EOF\\n$(kill 123)\\nEOF\" false\n\"echo $(kill 123)\" true"
   },
   "evidence": "signals.ts:28 tokenizes a whole double-quoted string as one token (`\"(?:\\\\.|[^\"\\\\])*\"`). That token is only matched as a command name and is never scanned again for $(...). signals.ts:19-25 replaces the body of any heredoc whose command is not sh or node/bun with nothing ('anything else (cat > file) is literal data'), but an unquoted heredoc still expands $(...). An unquoted substitution is detected only because the tokenizer splits on '('. The file calls itself 'Advisory recognition of ordinary executable forms', and these forms are unusual for an agent that is just reaching for pkill or kill."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An agent sends a signal inside a shell substitution in a double-quoted argument or an unquoted heredoc. Neither harness produces the required warning, so the attempt is absent from the warning feed and report count.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun \"$TMPDIR/adjudicate-signals-042.ts\"",
    "output": "{\"label\":\"C1 quoted substitution\",\"command\":\"echo \\\"$(kill -TERM 123)\\\"\",\"detected\":false,\"claudeWarnings\":0,\"codexWarnings\":0}\n{\"label\":\"C1 heredoc substitution\",\"command\":\"cat <<EOF\\n$(kill -TERM 123)\\nEOF\",\"detected\":false,\"claudeWarnings\":0,\"codexWarnings\":0}\nControl: kill -TERM 123 returned detected:true and one warning from each parser."
   },
   "evidence": "HEAD tokenizes an entire double-quoted argument as one token and subsequently skips it after recognizing echo. For heredocs, it scans the body only when SH.test(base) or JS.test(base) succeeds, then replaces the entire body with `${rest}\\n`. Shell command substitutions execute in both double-quoted arguments and unquoted heredocs regardless of the receiving command. The throwaway script passed command strings through the detector and both production harness parsers; it did not execute any signal commands. signals.ts does not exist at base."
  }
 },
