  "title": "Standard inline eval syntax silently bypasses signal warnings",
  "file": "src/harness/signals.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An agent runs node --eval=\"process.kill(123)\" with the equals form instead of a separate argument. The detector misses it and no warning is recorded, although node --eval \"...\" with a space is flagged.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun -e 'import { signalCommand } from \"./src/harness/signals.ts\"; console.log(signalCommand(\"node --eval=\\\"process.kill(123)\\\"\"), signalCommand(\"node --eval \\\"process.kill(123)\\\"\"))'",
    "output": "false true"
   },
   "evidence": "signals.ts:72 only accepts a flag token that exactly matches /^(?:-[ep]|--eval|--print)$/ and then reads the next token as the code. A token like --eval=CODE fails that match and is skipped as an ordinary option."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An agent uses the standard equals form of JavaScript's evaluation option to send a signal. Both harnesses miss the attempt, leaving the warning feed and report count unchanged.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun \"$TMPDIR/adjudicate-signals-042.ts\"",
    "output": "{\"label\":\"C3 inline eval\",\"command\":\"node --eval=\\\"process.kill(123)\\\"\",\"detected\":false,\"claudeWarnings\":0,\"codexWarnings\":0}\n{\"label\":\"control separate eval\",\"command\":\"node --eval \\\"process.kill(123)\\\"\",\"detected\":true,\"claudeWarnings\":1,\"codexWarnings\":1}"
   },
   "evidence": "HEAD extracts code only when an option matches the supplied flag regex exactly, returning the following token: `if (flag.test(tokens[j] ?? \"\")) return unquote(tokens[j + 1] ?? \"\");`. The JavaScript regex is `/^(?:-[ep]|--eval|--print)$/`, which excludes --eval=CODE. The production parser reproductions confirm that the missed detection suppresses warnings in both harnesses. This option parser is new relative to base."
  }
 },
